Explanation.

- ⚠️Beware though: these functions, like inject, are contextual functions. If you call them of a
    constructor, you’ll need to pass the component’s injector as an option.


🧠Let's understand what the above line means:

    🔥A contextual function is a function that only works when Angular knows which object (component,
    directive, service, etc.) is currently being created.
    For example,

        @Component({...})
        export class MyComponent {
            private service = inject(MyService);
        }

    ✅This works because, Angular is creating MyComponent. At that moment, Angular knows:
        -> which injector to use
        -> which providers are available
        -> what the current injection context is

    But

        @Component({...})
        export class MyComponent {
            constructor() {}

            load() {
                const service = inject(MyService);
            }
        }
    
    Then later

        const component = new MyComponent();
        component.load();

    ❌ This doesn't work because When load() runs, Angular is not creating anything anymore.
        -> There is no injection context.
        -> Angular has no idea which injector you mean.






    ⁉️ But Why does it work in field initializers?

            export class MyComponent {
                service = inject(MyService);
                logger = inject(LoggerService);
            }

        🧠because these initializers execute while Angular is constructing the component.
        🧠The injection context still exists.

    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    🫡Similar thing with the afterNextRender():
        For example:

            @Component({...})
            export class MyComponent {

                constructor() {
                    afterNextRender(() => {
                        console.log('Rendered!');
                    });
                }
            }

    ✅This works because you're calling afterNextRender() while Angular is constructing the
        component. Angular knows which component this callback belongs to.

    But 

        @Component({...})
        export class MyComponent {

            constructor() {}

            start() {
                afterNextRender(() => {
                    console.log('Rendered!');
                });
            }
        }
    
    Then later
    
        const component = new MyComponent();
        component.start();

    ❌ This doesn't work because Angular doesn't know which rendering context should own this
        callback. Just like inject(), afterNextRender() needs an injection context.
    
    🔥🔥 That's why the documentation says IF YOU CALL THEM OUTSIDE OF A CONSTRUCTOR, YOU'LL NEED
    TO PASS THE COMPONENT'S INJECTOR like:

        @Component({...})
        export class MyComponent {

            private injector = inject(Injector);

            start() {
                afterNextRender(() => { 
                        console.log('Rendered!'); 
                    }, 👉{ injector: this.injector }
                );
            }
        }
    
    ✅Now Angular knows exactly which injector (and therefore which component context) to use.